Reach the fallback for private-leader CSI sequences libvterm does not model

Two gaps in libvterm's own extension hook, both of which make sequences
unreachable rather than merely unhandled -- vterm_screen_set_unrecognised_
fallbacks never sees them, so an application cannot implement them itself:

  1. on_csi() rejects any leader byte other than '?' and '>' before the
     dispatch switch is reached, so `CSI < u` and `CSI = ... u` (the kitty
     keyboard protocol's pop and set controls) are dropped silently.

  2. `CSI ? 4 m` -- xterm's XTQMODKEYS query -- is consumed by the DECSGR
     case, a printer-era sequence no DEC terminal implemented, leaving a
     terminal unable to answer a query it does support.

Applied to the vendored copy at build time (see the top-level Makefile);
upstream is otherwise untouched. Worth sending upstream.

diff --git a/src/state.c b/src/state.c
index 54618d5..f8c495a 100644
--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -965,6 +965,8 @@ static int on_csi(const char *leader, const long args[], int argcount, const cha
     switch(leader[0]) {
     case '?':
     case '>':
+    case '<':
+    case '=':
       leader_byte = leader[0];
       break;
     default:
@@ -1376,6 +1378,14 @@ static int on_csi(const char *leader, const long args[], int argcount, const cha
     break;
 
   case LEADER('?', 0x6d): // DECSGR
+    /* Application-level fallback gets first refusal: XTQMODKEYS
+     * (CSI ? 4 m) has the same shape as DECSGR, and swallowing it here
+     * leaves a terminal unable to answer a query it does support.
+     */
+    if(state->fallbacks && state->fallbacks->csi)
+      if((*state->fallbacks->csi)(leader, args, argcount, intermed, command, state->fbdata))
+        return 1;
+
     /* No actual DEC terminal recognised these, but some printers did. These
      * are alternative ways to request subscript/superscript/off
      */
